### Worked example: why bytecode is not machine code

Suppose you compile:

```java
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(2 + 3);
    }
}
```

Step 1: `javac` compiles the source into a `.class` file.  
Step 2: the class file contains JVM bytecode and class metadata.  
Step 3: the JVM loads and verifies the class according to the JVM rules.  
Step 4: execution begins at the Java entry point used by the launcher.  
Step 5: the runtime may interpret instructions first and optimize hot code with JIT compilation.

This is why it is more accurate to say **Java bytecode is platform-independent input to a platform-specific JVM**, rather than saying the same machine code runs everywhere.

**Reference:** [Oracle JVM Specification](https://docs.oracle.com/javase/specs/jvms/se17/html/jvms-1.html)

