### Deep Dive: switch Statement vs switch Expression (Java 14+)

**Traditional switch statement (pre-Java 14) — fall-through risk:**
```java
switch (day) {
    case MONDAY:
    case TUESDAY:
        System.out.println("Weekday");
        break;           // MUST use break to prevent fall-through!
    case SATURDAY:
    case SUNDAY:
        System.out.println("Weekend");
        break;
    default:
        System.out.println("Unknown");
}
```

**Modern switch expression (Java 14+ finalized, JEP 361) — arrow syntax:**
```java
// No fall-through, no break needed, can return a value
String type = switch (day) {
    case MONDAY, TUESDAY, WEDNESDAY, THURSDAY, FRIDAY -> "Weekday";
    case SATURDAY, SUNDAY -> "Weekend";
    // No default needed if all enum cases are covered!
};
```

**Block body with yield (for complex cases):**
```java
int score = switch (grade) {
    case 'A' -> 4;
    case 'B' -> 3;
    case 'C' -> {
        System.out.println("Average grade");
        yield 2;  // yield returns a value from a block in switch expression
    }
    default -> 0;
};
```

**Key differences:**
| Feature | switch statement | switch expression |
|---------|-----------------|------------------|
| Fall-through | Yes (requires break) | No (arrow cases) |
| Return value | No | Yes |
| Exhaustiveness | Not required | Required (or default) |
| Multiple labels | One per case | Comma-separated |

**Further reading:**
- JEP 361: Switch Expressions — https://openjdk.org/jeps/361
- GeeksforGeeks: Java Switch — https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/java/switch-statement-in-java/

### Deep Dive: Variable Scope, Shadowing, and Lifetime

**Four scope levels in Java:**

```java
public class ScopeDemo {
    static int classVar = 0;    // Class scope — shared across all instances
    int instanceVar = 10;       // Instance scope — one per object

    void method() {
        int localVar = 20;      // Method scope — lives until method returns

        for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
            int blockVar = i * 2;  // Block scope — only inside this loop
            System.out.println(blockVar);
        }
        // i and blockVar are NOT accessible here

        System.out.println(localVar);     // OK
        System.out.println(instanceVar);  // OK
        System.out.println(classVar);     // OK
    }
}
```

**Shadowing rules:**
```java
public class Shadowing {
    int x = 100;           // instance field

    void method() {
        int x = 200;       // local variable SHADOWS the instance field
        System.out.println(x);       // 200 (local variable)
        System.out.println(this.x);  // 100 (instance field via 'this')
    }

    // Java PREVENTS local-to-local shadowing:
    void bad() {
        int y = 1;
        // int y = 2;  // COMPILE ERROR: y is already defined
        {
            // int y = 3;  // COMPILE ERROR: cannot shadow enclosing local
        }
    }
}
```

**Variable lifetime:**
- Local variables: allocated on stack frame, discarded when method returns
- Object fields: live as long as the object is reachable (on the heap)
- Static fields: live for the entire JVM session (class lifetime)

**Further reading:**
- W3Schools: Java Scope — https://www.w3schools.com/java/java_scope.asp
- GeeksforGeeks: Scope of Variables — https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/java/variable-scope-in-java/

